---
intent: >
  Implement the injectBadge init category — a helper that inspects the host
  project's README.md and returns an InitStep reporting whether the AIDE
  shields.io badge should be added, is already present, or cannot be added
  because no README exists. When called via the category parameter, it injects
  the badge line at the correct vertical position and returns a manifest entry.
---

## Plan

### 1. Register the "badge" category in types and orchestrator input schema

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`

- [x] In `src/types/index.ts`, add `"badge"` to the `InitCategory` union type (after `"ide"`).
- [x] In `src/tools/init/index.ts`, add `"badge"` to the `z.enum` array in the `category` field of `InitInput`.

### 2. Implement the injectBadge helper

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

- [x] Create `src/tools/init/injectBadge/index.ts` with a default export `injectBadge(projectRoot: string): Promise<InitStep>`.
- [x] The function receives the absolute project root path. It derives the README path as `join(projectRoot, "README.md")`.
- [x] Define the badge line as a constant string (the full `[![AIDE](...)](...)` Markdown image-link provided in the task).
- [x] Define the detection substring as the constant `img.shields.io/badge/AIDE` — used for the `includes()` idempotency check against file content.
- [x] Early return: if README.md does not exist, return an InitStep with `name: "README badge"`, `status: "would-skip"`, `category: "badge"`, `filePath: ""`. No content.
- [x] Read the README content. If it already contains the detection substring, return an InitStep with `status: "exists"`, `category: "badge"`, `filePath` set to the README path. No content.
- [x] Otherwise, compute the insertion position using the two-phase scan described in the spec: (a) skip YAML frontmatter if present (`---` opener through closing `---` plus any trailing blank line), (b) count consecutive lines starting with `[![` from the post-frontmatter position — this is the badge strip. Insert the badge line immediately after the last badge-strip line, or at the post-frontmatter position if no strip exists. Ensure a blank line separates the badge (or badge strip) from the content below.
- [x] Return an InitStep with `status: "would-create"`, `category: "badge"`, `filePath` set to the README path, and `content` set to the full modified README text. The `content` field carries the complete file so `applySteps` can write it directly.
- [x] JSDoc on the default export explaining the three-status contract (`would-skip` / `exists` / `would-create`) and the two-phase insertion logic.

### 3. Wire injectBadge into the init orchestrator

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

- [x] In `src/tools/init/index.ts`, import `injectBadge` from `./injectBadge/index.js`.
- [x] Call `injectBadge(projectRoot)` alongside the other category helpers and capture the returned step.
- [x] Add the badge step to the `steps` array. Place it after `vscodeStep` (end of the existing list) so the badge category appears last in the wizard — it is the least critical bootstrap step.

### 4. Write tests for injectBadge

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Create `src/tools/init/injectBadge/index.test.ts` with co-located unit tests using Vitest.
- [x] Mock `node:fs/promises` (`readFile`, `access` or equivalent) so no real filesystem access occurs.
- [x] Test: README does not exist — returns `status: "would-skip"`, empty `filePath`, no `content` field.
- [x] Test: README exists and already contains the detection substring — returns `status: "exists"`, correct `filePath`, no `content` field.
- [x] Test: README exists with no badges and no frontmatter — returns `status: "would-create"` with badge inserted at line 1, followed by a blank line before existing content.
- [x] Test: README exists with an existing badge strip (multiple consecutive `[![` lines) and no frontmatter — badge is appended after the last badge line, blank line preserved before heading.
- [x] Test: README exists with YAML frontmatter (`---` fences) and no badges — badge is inserted after frontmatter (after closing `---` and any blank line), not inside or before it.
- [x] Test: README exists with YAML frontmatter AND an existing badge strip — badge is appended after the badge strip, frontmatter untouched.
- [x] Use factory functions for building README content variations. Each test should build its input via a helper that accepts the README body string, keeping tests readable.

## Decisions

**"badge" as a new InitCategory value.** The spec explicitly requires the category
field to be registered in the InitCategory union and the orchestrator's z.enum.
A new category (rather than piggy-backing on "methodology" or "ide") gives the
agent a distinct discriminant to present badge status independently in the wizard.
This follows the existing pattern where each bootstrap concern gets its own
category value.

**Single-file helper, no subfolders.** The injectBadge logic is a single function
with no subhelpers worth extracting — the frontmatter skip and badge-strip scan
are a tight coupled sequence, not independent pipeline stages. This matches the
single-helper exception in progressive disclosure: when an orchestrator has
exactly one concern, the logic stays in one file. If the insertion logic later
grows (e.g., supporting multiple badge formats), it can be extracted then.

**Detection by URL substring, not full Markdown link.** The spec's strategy section
is explicit: `img.shields.io/badge/AIDE` is the durable identifier. This survives
style parameter changes, link text edits, and URL encoding variations. The
alternative — regex matching the full `[![AIDE](...)]` pattern — is brittle and
produces the duplicate-badge failure mode the spec calls out as undesired.

**Badge step placed last in the steps array.** The badge is a branding artifact,
not a structural bootstrap step. Placing it after IDE configuration means the
agent presents it last in the wizard, after all functional categories. This
follows the principle that the wizard walks categories in dependency order —
badge depends on nothing and nothing depends on badge.

**content field carries the full modified README.** This matches how applySteps
works for regular file steps — it writes `content` to `filePath`. The alternative
(returning just the badge line and having applySteps do the insertion) would
require applySteps to understand badge-specific insertion logic, breaking its
role as a generic file writer.

**filePath is empty string on would-skip.** When no README exists, there is no
target path. Using empty string (not undefined) matches the brain placeholder
pattern in the orchestrator where `filePath: ""` signals "no target" while
keeping the InitStep shape consistent.
